<section id="what">
  <h2>What a workspace is</h2>
  <p>Signing in for the first time creates a workspace called <strong>My Workspace</strong>, with you as its owner. Everything you connect belongs to it: repositories, pull requests, deployments and every metric derived from them.</p>
  <p>A workspace is <strong>not</strong> a GitHub organisation. One workspace can track repositories from several organisations and personal accounts at once, and two people can track the same repository in their own separate workspaces without either seeing the other's.</p>
  <p>Rename a workspace at any time from <strong>Settings → Team</strong>. Do it before inviting anyone — "My Workspace" is not a name a colleague will recognise in an invitation email.</p>
</section>

<section id="when-more">
  <h2>When you need more than one</h2>
  <p>Most teams don't. Reach for a second workspace when the <em>audience</em> differs, not when the repositories do — a workspace is a boundary for people as much as for data.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>An agency or consultancy</strong> keeping each client's delivery data separate.</li>
    <li><strong>Two organisations</strong> whose engineering leaders shouldn't see each other's numbers.</li>
    <li><strong>A personal sandbox</strong> alongside the company workspace you were invited to.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>To look at different repositories with the same people, don't create a workspace — add the repositories to the one you have and switch between them on the dashboard.</p>
</section>

<section id="managing">
  <h2>Creating and switching</h2>
  <p>Both live in the profile menu, top right.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Create workspace</strong> makes a new, empty one with you as its owner, ready for its own GitHub connections.</li>
    <li><strong>Switching</strong> reloads the dashboard with that workspace's data. Your choice is remembered for next time, and two browser tabs can sit in two different workspaces at once.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="leaving">
  <h2>Leaving and deleting</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Members</strong> can leave a workspace at any time from Settings → Team.</li>
    <li><strong>Owners cannot leave.</strong> Transfer ownership to another member first, then leave as a member.</li>
    <li><strong>A sole owner</strong> — an owner with no other members — can delete the workspace from Settings → Team. It removes the workspace and all of its data permanently, behind a confirmation. There is no undo and no export.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>Tried Distilled alone before your company adopted it?</strong> Accept the invitation to the company workspace, use the switcher to move between the two, then delete your personal one once you're settled. Deleting is available because you're its only user.</p>
  </div>
</section>

